### Metric definitions

| Metric | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Accuracy | Share of questions where the top option is the reference label |
| Macro-F1 | F1 per option label, averaged over labels |
| ECE | Expected calibration error: the average gap between confidence and accuracy over 15 equal-width confidence bins. Lower is better. |
| Brier | Squared error between the predicted distribution and the one-hot label, summed over options. Lower is better. |
| NLL | Negative log-likelihood of the reference label. Lower is better. |
| Coverage @5% error | The largest share of questions that can be decided automatically, most confident first, while keeping the error at or below 5% |
| Order flip | Share of questions whose answer changes when the option order is reversed |

## Prompt protocol

The protocol is documented in full in
[docs/protocol.md](https://github.com/{{ gh_repo }}/blob/main/docs/protocol.md). In short:

- One question per forward pass.
- Options are labelled with the letters `A`, `B`, and so on.
- In fast mode, the assistant turn starts with an empty think block.
- The probabilities are the softmax of the letter-token logits, divided by the calibrated
  temperature.

## Training

| | |
|---|---|
| Data | {{ "{:,}".format(stats.train_fast + stats.train_think) }} training records: {{ "{:,}".format(stats.train_fast) }} soft-label records and {{ "{:,}".format(stats.train_think) }} with teacher reasoning traces. They cover synthetic scenarios in 12 domains, BANKING77 and CLINC150. One trace longer than 2,048 tokens was skipped. See [nexus-decision-data](https://huggingface.co/datasets/{{ hf_user }}/nexus-decision-data). |
| Targets | Teacher soft distributions (top-20 logprobs, averaged over 3 option orders). For public data, they are mixed 50/50 with the gold one-hot label. |
| Reasoning traces | Kept only when the teacher's answer matched the target |
| Objective | KL(target ‖ student letter distribution) + 0.5 × LM loss on reasoning traces |
| LoRA | Rank {{ train.config.rank }}, alpha {{ train.config.alpha }}, on all linear projections in all {{ train.config.num_layers }} blocks, then merged into the base weights |
| Optimisation | AdamW, lr {{ "{:.0e}".format(train.config.lr) | replace("e-0", "e-") }}, cosine schedule, {{ train.config.epochs }} epochs, {{ "{:,}".format(train.step) }} steps, effective batch {{ train.config.batch_size * train.config.grad_accum }} |
| Hardware | {{ hardware }} |

## Limitations and responsible use

- **It decides on what the state says, literally.** Compute dates, totals and other arithmetic
  yourself, and put the results in the state.
- **Option order still has some effect.** For high-stakes questions, use `debias=True`.
- **The state is an attack surface.** Text inside the state can sway the model. Do not let
  untrusted state alone trigger privileged actions.
- **Confidence is not authorization.** Pick thresholds on your own labelled data, weighted by the
  cost of each kind of error, and always offer an abstain option.
- **Known weak spots.** Accuracy is lower on abstain cases, `score` questions and long option
  lists. See "Where it is stronger and weaker" above.
- **Language coverage.** Most training data is English. Each other language has fewer than 30
  test questions, so its figures in the breakdown are indicative only.
- **Synthetic labels.** These come from the teacher, so they inherit its mistakes and biases.

